    contentEn: `<h2>Getting from Palma Airport to Hotel Santos Nixe Palace</h2>
<p>A private transfer from Palma de Mallorca Airport (PMI) to Hotel Santos Nixe Palace covers about 12 kilometres and takes around 20 minutes by car, crossing Palma from the airport on the eastern side of the city to Cala Major, a bay a few kilometres west of the centre where the hotel sits close to the seafront. Your driver waits at arrivals with a name board, tracks your flight in real time so pickup adjusts automatically if you land early or late, and takes you straight to the hotel's entrance rather than leaving you to work out a taxi or bus connection along the coast road after a long flight.</p>
<p>The route runs from the airport along Palma's ring road, skirting the city centre before joining the coastal road that runs west through Cala Major toward Illetas. Because this stretch of coast is popular with both residents and visitors, particularly during the summer months, traffic along the seafront can build up at peak times, and a private driver familiar with the route generally manages this more smoothly than a shared shuttle making multiple stops. You can check live arrival information on <a href="https://www.aena.es">aena.es</a> before you fly, and arrange the whole journey — pickup point, flight number, and price — in advance through <a href="/get-a-quote/">get a quote</a>.</p>
<p>The same private service runs in reverse for your departure, with a driver collecting you from the hotel at the time you specify and taking you directly back to the terminal along the same coastal route. After a stay by the bay, having a car already arranged for the return journey means one less thing to organise on your last day in Mallorca.</p>
<h3>About Palma de Mallorca Airport</h3>
<p>Palma de Mallorca Airport (PMI), also known as Son Sant Joan Airport, is located around 8 kilometres east of Palma city and is Spain's third-busiest airport, a reflection of Mallorca's status as one of the country's leading holiday destinations. The airport handles a substantial number of flights throughout the year, with the busiest period running through the summer months when demand for onward transport is highest.</p>
<p>From the airport, reaching Cala Major means travelling across the full width of Palma, from the eastern coastal plain to the western bay, a journey that takes in both the city itself and the seafront road along the way.</p>
<h2>About Hotel Santos Nixe Palace</h2>
<p>Hotel Santos Nixe Palace stands in Cala Major, a bay a few kilometres west of central Palma that has long been a favoured stretch of coastline, historically associated with both local aristocracy and European royalty who kept residences along this part of the shore. The hotel takes its name from an unusual piece of local history connected directly to this bay.</p>
<p>The "Nixe" in the hotel's name refers to the yacht of Archduke Ludwig Salvator of Austria, a vessel that anchored in Cala Major bay in 1872. The Archduke was a noted admirer of Mallorca who spent decades studying and writing about the island, and the presence of his yacht in these waters became part of the area's identity, eventually lending its name to the hotel built here.</p>
<p>The hotel operates today under the name Hotel Santos Nixe Palace, reflecting its position within the Santos hotel group, and this current name is the one used across its operations and branding. Guests researching the property should note this is the name in current use, distinct from any earlier naming the hotel may have carried in past decades.</p>
<p>Cala Major itself remains a genuinely residential and seafront district rather than a purpose-built resort strip, giving the area around the hotel a settled, established character shaped by more than a century of history along this particular stretch of the bay.</p>
<h3>Who This Transfer Suits</h3>
<p>Travellers who want a seafront setting without being based directly in central Palma are the clearest fit for Hotel Santos Nixe Palace, given its position in Cala Major, close to the water. Guests interested in the story behind the bay's name and its connection to Archduke Ludwig Salvator will find a genuine piece of local history attached to their stay.</p>
<p>Because Cala Major sits a short drive from both central Palma and the airport, guests planning to split their time between the city and relaxing by the coast benefit from this location. Our <a href="/palma/">Palma</a> page has more on the wider city and its surrounding coastline.</p>
<h3>Cala Major and Its History</h3>
<p>Cala Major curves along the coast just west of Palma, with a promenade running above the shoreline and a mix of residential buildings, villas, and hotels facing the water. The bay's long association with Archduke Ludwig Salvator, whose yacht Nixe anchored here in 1872, is one of several threads connecting this part of the coast to Mallorca's nineteenth-century history.</p>
<p>The archduke's broader legacy on the island, including his extensive writings on Mallorca's landscape and traditions, is well documented in the island's history, and Cala Major's role as a mooring point for his yacht is a small but distinctive part of that wider story, now reflected in the hotel's name.</p>
<p>The promenade above the beach is a popular spot for an evening walk, with cafés and restaurants set back from the water along Cala Major's shoreline. Guests based at Hotel Santos Nixe Palace can reach this stretch on foot directly from the hotel, without needing to arrange separate transport for a simple walk along the coast.</p>
<h2>Getting There from the Airport: What to Expect</h2>
<p>Because the journey to Cala Major crosses the city and joins a coastal road that can see heavier traffic at peak times, particularly in summer, a pre-arranged private transfer takes the uncertainty out of timing your arrival. Drivers are available for early-morning and late-night arrivals alike, and flight tracking means a delayed landing does not leave you without transport.</p>
<p>Guests travelling with a child seat, beach equipment, or extra luggage should mention this when booking so the right vehicle is arranged in advance. This is worth planning ahead for, especially during the busier summer months when this stretch of coast sees the most visitors.</p>
<p>Business travellers visiting Palma for meetings elsewhere in the city will find the drive from Cala Major back into the centre just as manageable as the outbound journey from the airport, making the hotel a workable base beyond a purely leisure stay.</p>
<h2>Planning Your Transfer</h2>
<p>Whether your stay by Cala Major is a relaxed seaside break or a base for exploring Palma and beyond, booking both directions of your transfer in advance removes one more thing to think about once you land. See our <a href="/airport-transfers/">airport transfers</a> page for general arrival details, and if your itinerary includes other parts of Mallorca, our <a href="/hourly-chauffeur/">hourly chauffeur</a> service covers point-to-point travel whenever you need it.</p>`,
